Topic: {{topic}}

Steve's brief:
{{steve.current}}

Design the user experience for the first shippable version: user stories, information architecture, the critical path end to end, and the two or three moments where it could break. Hold your stance (user experience). At most 40 lines. Call design_turn(kind="design", text=...).

Topic: {{topic}}

Steve's brief:
{{steve.current}}

Design the visual layer: design system, colour and type roles, the key components, states, and the front-end constraints that follow (layout, density, what a terminal or web UI must do). Hold your stance (visual consistency and implementability). At most 40 lines. Call design_turn(kind="design", text=...).

Topic: {{topic}}

Steve's brief:
{{steve.current}}

Design the back end: technology choice with the reason, service and module split, data model, API surface, storage and deployment shape for the first version. Hold your stance (architectural soundness, no over-engineering). At most 40 lines. Call design_turn(kind="design", text=...).

Topic: {{topic}}

Steve's brief:
{{steve.current}}

Design the algorithmic core: which algorithm, complexity, accuracy target, the fallback when it fails, and a feasibility check (data needed, offline vs online). If there is no real algorithm in this product, say so in two lines and pivot to the hardest technical risk instead. Hold your stance (feasibility). At most 40 lines. Call design_turn(kind="design", text=...).

Topic: {{topic}}

Steve's brief:
{{steve.current}}

=== UX ===
{{design_ux.current}}

=== UI ===
{{design_ui.current}}

=== BACKEND ===
{{design_backend.current}}

=== ALGO ===
{{design_algo.current}}

Unify these into ONE executable plan: milestones, task breakdown (each task small enough for one developer turn), schedule and dependencies, tech choices, and the module file layout. Where a designer asked for something you cannot schedule, cut it or defer it and say so in one line (you hold the executability stance). At most 80 lines. Call design_turn(kind="plan", text=...).

